            },
            "text": "np.astype converts an array to a different number type.\n\nAn array of float64 and one of float32 hold the same numbers and are not interchangeable everywhere - image work usually wants uint8, torch usually wants float32, and indices have to be integers. When a node refuses an array that looks correct, the type is a likely culprit, and info will tell you what it actually is.\n\nTHE TYPES:\nChosen from the \"type\" menu: bool, uint8, int8, int64, float, float32, double. There is no int32 here. \"float\" and \"double\" are the same thing, the 64-bit float; \"float32\" is the single-precision one torch usually wants.\n\nCONVERTING TO INTEGERS TRUNCATES:\nConverting a float array to an integer type cuts off the fraction rather than rounding, so 9.7 becomes 9 and -0.9 becomes 0. If you want the nearest whole number, round first with np.round and then convert.\n\nOut-of-range values do not stop at the limit: converted to uint8, 300 comes out as 44 and -1 as 255. Clip first (np.clip) if the data can leave the 0 to 255 range.\n\nLists and single numbers are accepted too, and come out as arrays.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.astype\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.astype\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput array:\nThe array. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\ntype:\nWhat to convert to.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nconverted array:\nThe same values, in the new type."
        },
